// v3 substrate reflection — the substrate's own types as .dag
// declarations, declared in the substrate's own language.
//
// **What this file is.** The reflection surface described in
// docs/substrate-reflection-design.md §3.1. Every structural
// substrate type the compiler carries (Dag, Declaration,
// Behavior, TypeConnective, ArrowBody, AtomPayload, …) is declared
// here as an ordinary `.dag` Conj / Disj declaration. The Rust
// structs in src/v3/compiler/src/dag.rs are the realizations
// of these declarations — kept consistent via the inhabitance
// check that runs at bootstrap time. There is no parallel
// authority: the .dag form is the structural declaration,
// the Rust form is its realization.
//
// **Why `src/v3/std/` instead of `dsl/std/`.** v2's CI pipeline
// scans `dsl/` recursively and tries to resolve every identifier
// in every record-literal field value. v2 doesn't know about v3
// substrate types, so placing this file in `dsl/std/substrate.dag`
// would break v2 CI. `src/v3/std/` is the bootstrap staging
// location during the v2→v3 window; the canonical eventual home
// is `dsl/std/substrate.dag` once v2 retires (see THESIS.md
// §"Two groundings" — bootstrap staging note). Same pattern
// `src/v3/spec/rust.dag` uses relative to its eventual home at
// `dsl/extdeps/languages/rust.dag`.
//
// **Load order.** `src/v3/compiler/build.rs` scans this
// directory and orders the substrate files after `v3_l1.dag`
// (the substrate marker file) and before any per-target
// language spec (e.g., `rust.dag`). Two-phase bootstrap resolves
// cross-file references regardless of order, but the convention
// keeps the dependency direction readable.
//
// **Seed minimality** (docs/substrate-reflection-design.md §3.0
// + §7.2a): the only concepts that remain Rust-native seed
// primitives are the parser, the resolve sweep, the arithmetic
// primitives (Int / Bool / String), and the four atomic identity
// handles NodeId / PortId / DeclarationId / SourceSpan. Every
// other substrate concept lives as a .dag declaration in this
// file (and its siblings), realized by the Rust structs in
// dag.rs.

module v3.std.substrate

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// §1. Atomic identity handles.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// These are **opaque atoms** — declarations with no structural
// body. They exist in `.dag` so every identifier in the substrate
// surface has a name to resolve against; their Rust backing types
// (`NodeId(u32)`, `PortId(u32)`, `DeclarationId(u32)` — newtype
// wrappers in `src/v3/compiler/src/dag.rs`) are attached via
// `TypeRealization` entries in `src/v3/spec/rust.dag` the same
// way `Int → i64` is wired.
//
// Semantically these are identity tokens: a `.dag` program can
// compare two NodeIds for equality and pass them around as
// handles, but never inspect their internal shape. A lens that
// needs to turn a NodeId into a Behavior goes through the query
// primitive `node_by_id(dag, id)` (declared in a sibling file);
// it cannot decompose the handle itself.
//
// SourceSpan is not re-declared here — it already has a
// structural record declaration in `dsl/std/types.dag` that both
// v2 and v3 can see, so it's reachable through the existing
// cross-file reference mechanism.

type NodeId
type PortId
type DeclarationId

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// §2. Leaf structural types.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Types with no recursive self-reference and few / trivial
// dependencies. Declared before the larger sums that reference
// them so a reader of this file can read top-to-bottom and see
// each concept defined before it's used. Two-phase bootstrap
// would also accept any order, but top-down reading is clearer.

// Literal scalar payload carried at the type level (e.g. the
// `3` in `Cardinality::Exact(3)`). Matches Rust's
// `LiteralBits` enum in dag.rs — the three variant names are
// qualified (`LitInt`, `LitBool`, `LitString`) to avoid
// visually shadowing the `Int` / `Bool` / `String` primitive
// type names at read time. (Variant names are not top-level
// symbols in v3, so there is no actual resolution collision;
// the rename is purely for readability.)
type LiteralBits
  = LitInt(Int)
  | LitBool(Bool)
  | LitString(String)

// Atom payload variant — the 4-way sum describing a
// TypeConnective::Atom's inner content. Mirrors Rust's
// `AtomPayload` in dag.rs.
type AtomPayload
  = Literal(LiteralBits)
  | UnresolvedIdentifier(String)
  | ResolvedIdentifier(DeclarationId)
  | TypeParam(String)

// CardinalityBound — the "how many?" dimension of field /
// element repetition. 3 variants. Matches Rust's
// `CardinalityBound` in dag.rs.
type CardinalityBound
  = Exact(Int)
  | AtMostOne
  | Unbounded

// ConjField — one label:type slot on a Conj. The substrate's
// `Field` name (per §3.1 of the design doc) is adapted to
// `ConjField` here to avoid collision with `dsl/std/algebra.dag`'s
// `Field<T>` (the mathematical field / division ring with
// reciprocal and divide). Both names are legitimate for their
// respective domains; the collision is avoided by name, not by
// semantic conflation. Matches Rust's `Field` struct in dag.rs.
type ConjField {
  label: String
  ty: DeclarationId
}

// TemplateArgument — binds a template parameter to a concrete
// type. Matches Rust's `TemplateArgument` in dag.rs.
type TemplateArgument {
  parameter: DeclarationId
  value: DeclarationId
}

// FieldValue — per-field payload inside a ValueBody::Structural.
// Each field carries either a scalar literal or a typed
// reference to another declaration. Matches Rust's `FieldValue`
// in dag.rs.
type FieldValue
  = FieldLiteral(LiteralBits)
  | FieldReference(DeclarationId)

// One (label, value) entry in a Structural value body. Rust
// represents these as `Vec<(String, FieldValue)>`; .dag has no
// tuple type so the pair is hoisted to a named record. Same
// information, different container shape.
type NamedFieldValue {
  label: String
  value: FieldValue
}

// ValueBody — the body of a `data foo: T = { ... }`
// declaration. Matches Rust's `ValueBody` in dag.rs.
type ValueBody
  = ValueBodyUnparsed(SourceSpan)
  | ValueBodyStructural { fields: List<NamedFieldValue> }

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// §3. Type substrate.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ArrowBody — mixed-lifecycle 4-variant coproduct describing
// how an Arrow's body is carried. Matches Rust's `ArrowBody`
// in dag.rs. Terminal form is `UserDefined` / `ExternalRealization`;
// `Pending` / `Unparsed` are scaffold variants with named
// dissolution triggers in the Rust dissolution ledger.
type ArrowBody
  = UserDefined(NodeId)
  | ExternalRealization(DeclarationId)
  | Pending
  | Unparsed(SourceSpan)

// TypeConnective — the 6-variant type substrate. Matches
// Rust's `TypeConnective` in dag.rs. Terminal at M1(2.5).
type TypeConnective
  = Atom(AtomPayload)
  | Conj { children: List<ConjField> }
  | Disj { variants: List<ConjField> }
  | Arrow {
      inputs: List<DeclarationId>
      output: DeclarationId
      body: ArrowBody
    }
  | Cardinality {
      element: DeclarationId
      bound: CardinalityBound
    }
  | Instantiation {
      template: DeclarationId
      arguments: List<TemplateArgument>
    }

// Declaration — the unit of the type substrate. Matches Rust's
// `Declaration` struct in dag.rs. Free from `DeclarationRef`
// (the v3_l1.dag sentinel) since the rename that preceded this
// commit; this is the canonical name for "a declaration in
// the substrate's type table."
//
// Note on Option<T> fields: v3 uses `T?` for optional types.
// Rust's `Option<DeclarationId>` lowers to
// Cardinality::AtMostOne here, matching types.dag's
// `expires_at: Timestamp?` pattern.
type Declaration {
  id: DeclarationId
  name: String
  connective: TypeConnective
  type_params: List<DeclarationId>
  meta_tag: DeclarationId?
  inhabits: DeclarationId?
  value_body: ValueBody?
  span: SourceSpan
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// §4. Computation substrate — the five L1 behaviors.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Each L1 behavior is a distinct record shape. Matching Rust's
// concrete node types (ValueNode, TransformNode, …) lets the
// inhabitance check do field-by-field drift detection against
// dag.rs rather than having to flatten the variant into an
// abstract shape.

// Per-Branch-arm pattern. Matches Rust's `BranchPattern` phase
// coproduct.
type BranchPattern
  = UnresolvedVariant { name: String, span: SourceSpan }
  | ResolvedVariant(DeclarationId)

// Path through a Branch. Named `BranchPath` here to avoid the
// collision with `dsl/std/types.dag`'s `Path = String where
// non_empty` (a refinement type for filesystem paths). Rust's
// dag.rs calls this `Path`; the rename is cosmetic and the
// inhabitance check will look up this declaration by its role
// (a field of BranchNode), not by Rust name.
type BranchPath {
  body: NodeId
  output: PortId
  pattern: BranchPattern
}

// Loop bound — carries the iteration count port. Matches
// Rust's `Bound` struct in dag.rs. Renamed to `LoopBound` here
// because `Bound` is a generic name; `LoopBound` makes clear
// which substrate concept owns this shape.
type LoopBound {
  count: PortId
}

// Value behavior — a literal carried as a node.
//
// **Field elision.** The Rust `ValueNode` struct carries a
// `data: LiteralBits` field (the literal's bit pattern). The
// v3 tokenizer reserves `data` as a keyword for `data foo: T
// = { ... }` item declarations, so `data: LiteralBits` cannot
// appear as a field name inside a `type` record at M1(3). The
// .dag declaration below omits the field pending one of three
// dissolution triggers:
//   (a) parser extension for quoting keyword-name fields
//       (e.g. `\`data\`: LiteralBits`), or
//   (b) rename the Rust field to `payload` (~10 sites: two
//       consumers in `emit_rust.rs` / `infer.rs`, tests in
//       `m0_acceptance.rs`, the `dag.rs` doc comment), or
//   (c) expose `value_node_data(v: ValueNode) -> LiteralBits`
//       as a query primitive that sidesteps the field view
//       entirely.
// Tracked as a substrate reflection follow-up; the current
// lens needing reflection (`lens_unused_parameters`) does not
// read `ValueNode.data`, so the elision doesn't block the
// reflection surface's first use case.
type ValueNode {
  id: NodeId
  output: PortId
  span: SourceSpan
}

// Transform behavior — one Apply of a target Arrow.
//
// **Terminal-form target.** Rust's TransformNode carries
// `target: TransformTarget` where TransformTarget is a mixed-
// lifecycle 2-variant coproduct: `Callable(DeclarationId)` is
// terminal, `Operator(OperatorKind)` is a scheduled-to-dissolve
// scaffold (the dissolution trigger per the M1(2.7) dispatch
// receipt is the M2+ parser / desugarer collapsing Operator
// back into Callable via direct algebra-field calls). The .dag
// substrate declares `target: DeclarationId` directly — the
// post-dissolution shape — so the reflection surface shows the
// terminal form. The scaffold variant is not observable through
// the reflection surface; a lens that inspects a TransformNode
// with a live Operator(...) target sees the DeclarationId of
// the resolved operator field (not the Rust OperatorKind enum),
// which is the same information the inference layer resolves at
// dispatch time.
//
// This is not the same as "ignore the scaffold": the scaffold
// still exists in Rust, but the inhabitance check treats the
// .dag declaration as the authoritative terminal shape, and
// the Rust→.dag drift here is a tracked dissolution remainder,
// not a substrate bug.
type TransformNode {
  id: NodeId
  target: DeclarationId
  inputs: List<PortId>
  output: PortId
  span: SourceSpan
}

// Branch behavior — coproduct elimination with one Path per
// arm.
type BranchNode {
  id: NodeId
  input: PortId
  paths: List<BranchPath>
  output: PortId
  span: SourceSpan
}

// Loop behavior — well-founded recursion with a bound.
type LoopNode {
  id: NodeId
  source: PortId
  init: PortId
  body: NodeId
  bound: LoopBound
  output: PortId
  span: SourceSpan
}

// Bind behavior — let-abstraction. Function bindings have
// non-empty `params`; value bindings have empty `params`
// (C2 dissolution receipt in dag.rs).
type BindNode {
  id: NodeId
  name: String
  value: PortId
  params: List<PortId>
  span: SourceSpan
}

// The 5 L1 behaviors as a sum. Matches Rust's `Behavior` enum.
// Variant names are NOT registered as top-level declarations
// in v3's flat symbol table (parser only registers top-level
// items), so there is no collision with v3_l1.dag's
// `type Value {}` / `type Transform {}` / etc. substrate
// markers.
type Behavior
  = Value(ValueNode)
  | Transform(TransformNode)
  | Branch(BranchNode)
  | Loop(LoopNode)
  | Bind(BindNode)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// §5. The Dag container.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Dag — the substrate's top-level container. Holds the
// declaration table and the behavior table. Matches Rust's
// `Dag` struct in dag.rs (minus the private caches:
// substrate_markers, realization_metas, primitives — those are
// internal acceleration structures, not part of the semantic
// substrate surface).
type Dag {
  declarations: List<Declaration>
  nodes: List<Behavior>
}
